'''Vinod Goenka''' (born 2 July 1959) is an Indian [[real-estate developer]] and [[billionaire]]. He is Chairman and Managing Director of Valor Estate (formerly DB Realty Limited), a real estate development company headquartered in [[Mumbai]], India.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mumbai: Vinod Goenka to inquire with CP about missing brother |url=https://www.freepressjournal.in/mumbai/mumbai-vinod-goenka-to-inquire-with-cp-about-missing-brother |access-date=2023-05-30 |website=Free Press Journal |language=en}}</ref>

In 2010, his net worth was estimated at US$1.18 billion.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Vinod Goenka |url=https://www.forbes.com/profile/vinod-goenka/ |access-date=2024-10-10 |website=Forbes |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=In Pictures: The Richest People In The World |url=https://www.forbes.com/2010/03/09/worlds-richest-people-slim-gates-buffett-billionaires-2010-intro_slide.html |access-date=2025-09-01 |website=Forbes |language=en}}</ref>

== Early life and education ==

Goenka was born in 1959 in Kolkata. When he was 6 years old, his family moved to Mumbai. He first attended Green Lawns High School, then [[Jamnabai Narsee School]], and eventually finished his schooling at [[Campion School, Mumbai]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2 April 1990 |title=Vinod Goenka Class of '76, Old Campionite's Association {{!}} Official website of the Old Campionite's Association |url=https://oldsite.campionites.com/spotlight_detail.html?Id=96 |access-date=2025-09-01 |website=oldsite.campionites.com}}</ref> He competed in Track and Field and narrowly missed qualifying for the [[Asian Games]]. An injury later led him to shift focus away from sports.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=28 April 2025 |title=How Vinod Kumar Goenka Built a Multi-Dimensional Business Legacy |url=https://www.mid-day.com/buzz/article/how-vinod-kumar-goenka-built-a-multi-dimensional-business-legacy-5790 |access-date=2025-04-29 |website=[[Mid-day]]}}</ref>

== Career ==
At just 19 years old, he began working in his father's business in the 1980s. In the early 1980s, Goenka started his real estate empire with Conwood Constructions and launched projects such as [[Gokuldham]] and [[Yashodham]] in [[Goregaon]], [[Mahavir Nagar (Kandivali)|Mahavir Nagar]] in [[Kandivli]], Vasant Vihar in [[Thane]] and Shristi in [[Mira Road]].<ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Saini |first=Manish |date=2024-06-19 |title=Vinod Kumar Goenka's Quest To Build Communities And Create Icons |url=https://www.outlookindia.com/hub4business/vinod-kumar-goenkas-quest-to-build-communities-and-create-icons |access-date=2024-10-10 |website=Outlook India |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=24 October 2024 |title=Vinod Goenka and Valor Estate: A journey of innovation |url=https://www.zeebiz.com/agencies/vinod-goenka-and-valor-estate-a-journey-of-innovation-323053 |access-date=2026-02-06 |website=Zee Business |language=en}}</ref> In 1994, he diversified his business by venturing into the dairy industry in partnership with the US-based [[Schreiber Foods]], establishing an integrated dairy plant in [[Baramati]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Babbar |first=Kailash |date=2011-04-21 |title=From boardroom to courtroom: India Inc high-fliers behind bars in 2G scam |url=https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/telecom/from-boardroom-to-courtroom-india-inc-high-fliers-behind-bars-in-2g-scam/articleshow/8043471.cms?from=mdr |access-date=2024-12-23 |work=The Economic Times |issn=0013-0389}}</ref> By 2006, he partnered with [[Shahid Balwa]], another Mumbai-based realtor in a 50:50 collaboration to develop Le Méridien Mumbai (now Hilton International Airport Hotel). This partnership resulted in the creation of the DB group.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Kamath |first=Raghvendra |date=25 January 2013 |title=The ups and downs in the DB Realty story |url=https://www.business-standard.com/article/markets/the-ups-and-downs-in-the-db-realty-story-111021000113_1.html |access-date=10 October 2024 |work=[[Business Standard]]}}</ref> In a few years, the company reported 21 million sq ft under construction and 40 million sq ft in planned projects.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Behera |first=Bidyadhar |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=p-mcDwAAQBAJ&dq=%22Vinod+Goenka%22&pg=PA1 |title=Entrepreneurship Text and cases |date=2019-06-12 |publisher=MJP Publisher |isbn=978-81-8094-215-0 |location=[[Chennai]] |page=37 |language=en}}</ref>

[[File:Gokuldham_5.jpg|left|thumb|250px|Skyline view of [[Gokuldham]] in Goregaon in 2009]]

Goenka, known for [[cluster development]] in Mumbai and its suburbs, focuses on redeveloping aging buildings and converting them into [[Affordable housing in India|affordable housing]] while adhering to sustainable urban growth and town planning goals.<ref name=":2" /> In 2020, the [[Government of Maharashtra]] established a steering committee comprising cabinet ministers, senior officials, and industry leaders, including developers like Goenka to address real estate challenges and support initiatives like these.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Vyas |first=Sharad |date=2020-02-10 |title=Committee to look into real estate woes |url=https://www.thehindu.com/news/cities/mumbai/committee-to-look-into-real-estate-woes/article30787354.ece |access-date=2024-11-16 |work=The Hindu |language=en-IN |issn=0971-751X}}</ref>

As of January 2025, Goenka is involved in redevelopment projects in and around Mumbai, including slums, cooperative societies, and [[Maharashtra Housing and Area Development Authority]]-owned properties. Since beginning his work in slum redevelopment in 1995, he has been part of projects totaling over 1 million square feet. His company holds over 500 acres in Mumbai<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nandy |first=Madhurima |date=2025-09-16 |title=Battered by 2G, written off by rivals—DB Realty is now shaping Mumbai's skyline |url=https://www.livemint.com/industry/db-realty-comeback-valor-estate-mumbai-slum-redevelopment-11758013779304.html |access-date=2025-10-03 |website=mint |language=en}}</ref> and is currently involved in Ten BKC, a five-acre redevelopment project near [[Bandra Kurla Complex]], developed in collaboration with Adani GoodHomes.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Malhotra |first=Khushi |date=2025-01-15 |title=Valor Estate, Adani set to deliver long-awaited landmark BKC project in June {{!}} Company Business News |url=https://www.livemint.com/companies/news/real-estate-db-realty-valor-estate-adani-goodhomes-bkc-project-ten-bkc-redevelopment-projects-11736923924590.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250124014907/https://www.livemint.com/companies/news/real-estate-db-realty-valor-estate-adani-goodhomes-bkc-project-ten-bkc-redevelopment-projects-11736923924590.html |archive-date=24 January 2025 |access-date=2025-01-26 |work=[[Mint (newspaper)|Mint]] |language=en |url-status=live }}</ref>

== Philanthropy ==
Vinod Goenka heads Goenka & Associates Educational Trust (GAET), which operates eight schools in Mumbai and Thane and is developing Goa’s first International Baccalaureate school. Collectively, these schools have over 22,000 students enrolled.<ref name=":1" />

==Controversies==
Goenka was arrested on 20 April 2011 for his alleged involvement in the [[2G spectrum case]]. During his imprisonment, he temporarily stepped down from his roles in the companies he was associated with.<ref>{{cite news |title=2G: Vinod Goenka moves HC against charge-framing |url=http://www.newindianexpress.com/nation/article335881.ece |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141009172029/http://www.newindianexpress.com/nation/article335881.ece |archive-date=9 October 2014 |access-date=6 October 2014 |issue=24–01–12 |agency=Indian express}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |author=PTI |date=24 January 2012 |title=2G case: Vinod Goenka moves HC against framing of charges |url=https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/economy/2g-case-vinod-goenka-moves-hc-against-framing-of-charges/article2828099.ece |work=The Hindu Business Line}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |date=14 April 2015 |title=2G scam witness names Balwa, Goenka |url=https://www.deccanherald.com/content/471519/2g-scam-witness-names-balwa.html |work=Deccan Herald}}</ref><ref name=":0" />  After spending over seven months in custody, Goenka was granted bail by the [[Supreme Court of India]] on 23 November 2011.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-12-22 |title=2G scam verdict: Time spent in jail by those acquitted in the case |url=https://indianexpress.com/article/india/2g-scam-verdict-time-spent-in-jail-by-those-acquitted-in-the-case-4994451/ |access-date=2025-01-26 |website=The Indian Express |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Mishra |first=Lalatendu |date=26 April 2011 |title=Families step in to rescue DB Realty Ltd from going under |url=https://www.businesstoday.in/current/corporate/families-step-in-to-rescue-db-realty/story/15079.html |website=[[Business Today (India)|Business Today]]}}</ref> In 2013, UAE telecommunications operator [[Etisalat by e&|Etisalat]] initiated legal proceedings against its Indian partners, Goenka and Shahid Balwa, after the Supreme Court canceled the latter's 2G licenses.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.business-standard.com/article/companies/etisalat-sues-vinod-goenka-shahid-balwa-for-fraud-112022300178_1.html|title=Etisalat sues Vinod Goenka, Shahid Balwa for fraud|agency=Reuters|date=23 February 2012}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ghosh |first=Shauvik |title=Etisalat sues partners Balwa, Goenka over telecom fiasco - Livemint |url=https://www.livemint.com/Companies/releyXX8SOQQuUoKzmRygJ/Etisalat-sues-partners-Balwa-Goenka-over-telecom-fiasco.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180703225325/https://www.livemint.com/Companies/releyXX8SOQQuUoKzmRygJ/Etisalat-sues-partners-Balwa-Goenka-over-telecom-fiasco.html |archive-date=3 July 2018 |access-date=2025-01-26 |website=[[Mint (newspaper)|Mint]] |date=23 February 2012 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=2012-02-23 |title=Etisalat takes Swan Telecom promoters to court |url=https://www.thehindu.com/business/etisalat-takes-swan-telecom-promoters-to-court/article2924946.ece |access-date=2025-01-26 |work=[[The Hindu]] |language=en-IN |issn=0971-751X}}</ref> On 21 December 2017, Goenka and all 18 other accused persons in the 2G spectrum case were acquitted of all charges levied against them.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.timesnownews.com/business-economy/companies/video/justice-has-been-done-says-vinod-goenka-on-2g-scam-verdict/180877|title=Justice has been done, says Vinod Goenka on 2G scam verdict|date=21 December 2017 |agency=ET Now|issue=21–12–17}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/naazneenkarmali/2017/12/21/in-landmark-verdict-court-acquits-billionaire-ruia-and-others-in-indias-telecom-corruption-case/#14ab6ed32738|title=In Landmark Verdict, Court Acquits Billionaire Ruia And Others In India's Telecom Corruption Case|agency=Forbes|issue=21–12–17|author=Naazneen Karmali|website=[[Forbes]] }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/mumbai/justice-done-says-realty-co-chairman-after-2g-acquittal/articleshow/62201305.cms|title=Justice done, says realty co-chairman after 2G acquittal|website=[[The Times of India]] |date=22 December 2017 |agency=TimesofIndia|issue=22–12–17}}</ref>

Goenka was linked to a [[Yes Bank]]-[[Dewan Housing Finance Corporation]] fraud case over a ₹350 crore loan his firm, Neelkamal Realtors, received from Yes Bank between 2013 and 2016, which the [[Central Bureau of Investigation|Central Blook-out Investigation]] alleged was diverted as part of a ₹4,733 crore siphoning scheme involving DHFL.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bharucha |first=Nauzer |date=2021-03-01 |title=DHFL's Rs 7,500 crore funds stuck in 8 big Mumbai realty projects |url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/mumbai/dhfls-rs-7500-crore-funds-stuck-in-8-big-mum-realty-projects/articleshow/81264375.cms |access-date=2025-03-28 |work=The Times of India |issn=0971-8257}}</ref> Despite raids in 2022 and a chargesheet in 2024,<ref>{{Cite web |title=DHFL-Yes Bank Scam: CBI Recovers Incriminating Documents in Raids |url=https://www.moneylife.in/article/dhflyes-bank-scam-cbi-recovers-incriminating-documents-in-raids/67049.html |access-date=2025-03-28 |website=Moneylife NEWS & VIEWS |language=en}}</ref> a CBI court in March 2025 dropped the charges against Goenka, finding that the loan had been settled with [[Piramal Capital and Housing Finance Limited]], which had acquired DHFL, before the chargesheet was filed and that there was no evidence of fraud, thereby clearing him of liability. This repayment was a key factor in the court's decision to dismiss the allegations against him.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Rajput |first=Rashmi |date=2025-03-15 |title=Yes Bank-DHFL case: Charges against Shahid Balwa, Vinod Goenka dropped |url=https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/banking/finance/banking/yes-bank-dhfl-case-charges-against-shahid-balwa-vinod-goenka-dropped/articleshow/119052548.cms?from=mdr |access-date=2025-03-28 |work=The Economic Times |issn=0013-0389}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Suresh |first=Revu |date=16 March 2025 |title=Lack of evidence: CBI court drops charges against key accused in Yes Bank-DHFL fraud case |url=https://www.hindustantimes.com/cities/mumbai-news/lack-of-evidence-cbi-court-drops-charges-against-key-accused-in-yes-bank-dhfl-fraud-case-101742063671517.html |access-date=28 March 2025 |work=[[The Hindustan Times]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |title=Court rejects chargesheet against Goenka, Balwa in Yes Bank-DHFL case |url=https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/court-rejects-chargesheet-against-goenka-balwa-in-yes-bank-dhfl-case-125031500610_1.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250317083418/https://www.business-standard.com/companies/news/court-rejects-chargesheet-against-goenka-balwa-in-yes-bank-dhfl-case-125031500610_1.html |archive-date=17 March 2025 |access-date=2025-03-28 |website=www.business-standard.com |language=en-US |url-status=live }}</ref> In distinct rulings, the court also instructed the CBI to withdraw the [[look out circular]] issued against Goenka.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2025-03-15 |title=Yes Bank-DHFL case: Court refuses to take cognisance of chargesheet against Goenka, Balwa |url=https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/banking/finance/banking/yes-bank-dhfl-case-court-refuses-to-take-cognisance-of-chargesheet-against-goenka-balwa/articleshow/119049630.cms?from=mdr |access-date=2025-07-02 |work=The Economic Times |issn=0013-0389}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-03-15 |title=Yes Bank-DHFL loan case: Court says no case made out against nine including Balwa |url=https://indianexpress.com/article/cities/mumbai/yes-bank-dhfl-loan-court-says-no-case-against-nine-including-balwa-9887866/ |access-date=2025-07-02 |website=The Indian Express |language=en}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Goenka resides in [[Mumbai]]. He is married to Aseela Goenka and has two children, a son and a daughter.<ref>{{cite news |date=30 April 2013 |title=2G case: Vinod Goenka's wife tells court her husband did not commit any illegality |url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/2G-case-Vinod-Goenkas-wife-tells-court-her-husband-did-not-commit-any-illegality/articleshow/19793726.cms |work=The Times of India}}</ref>

